Use oversized editorial headlines, carefully controlled letter spacing, fluid typography, generous whitespace, fine borders, and precise alignment.

Avoid excessive neon, generic cyberpunk clutter, unnecessary glassmorphism cards, cartoon-style 3D objects, and repetitive animation effects.

### 3. REQUIRED TECHNOLOGY STACK

Build the application with:

- React and Vite
- JavaScript or TypeScript, preferably TypeScript for a maintainable implementation
- Three.js for real-time 3D rendering
- React Three Fiber for declarative 3D scenes
- Drei for useful Three.js helpers and environment utilities
- GSAP and ScrollTrigger for timeline animation and scroll-driven storytelling
- Lenis for smooth scrolling, integrated correctly with GSAP and ScrollTrigger
- React Router for functional page navigation and page transitions
- CSS Modules or a well-organized global CSS architecture
- Lucide React for consistent interface icons

Use custom GLSL shaders where they materially enhance the visual experience.

Use genuine 3D geometry, physically based materials, lighting, reflections, camera choreography, and procedural shaders wherever appropriate.

Prefer a real product model in GLB or GLTF format when an appropriate, legally usable asset is available. If one is unavailable, create a convincing product from procedural geometry and custom materials. Never rely on a missing external model for the website to function.

Do not claim to use a 3D model, library, API, or external service unless it has actually been implemented.

### 4. WEBSITE ARCHITECTURE

Create these routes:

- `/` — immersive cinematic home
- `/product` — detailed product experience and customization
- `/technology` — materials, engineering, and technical storytelling
- `/craftsmanship` — product construction and design philosophy
- `/checkout` — functional demo cart review and checkout flow

All routes must render correctly when opened directly or refreshed. Navigation should update the browser history and support the back and forward buttons.

The site must work as one coherent branded experience, sharing the same design system, navigation, product configuration, and cart state.

### 5. CINEMATIC HOMEPAGE

#### Section A: Immersive intro loader

Design an elegant loading sequence with:

- AURELION brand mark
- Minimal progress indicator
- Technical loading labels
- Fine animated lines
- A subtle light sweep
- Smooth reveal into the hero

Show genuine loading progress when it is measurable. Otherwise, use a short, interruptible visual intro rather than displaying fabricated percentages.

Do not let the loader block the website indefinitely. Implement a timeout, WebGL failure fallback, and reduced-motion alternative.

The loader should only display on initial loading or when useful, not unnecessarily on every interaction.

#### Section B: Full-screen 3D hero

Build an immersive hero section with a real interactive 3D product at the center or slightly off-center.

The product should appear suspended in a dark environment, illuminated by cinematic rim lights with subtle reflections, controlled bloom, and atmospheric depth.

Use:
- A perspective camera with carefully controlled framing
- Physically based materials
- Studio-style area lighting or suitable simulated studio lights
- A dark environmental background
- Procedural floating particles
- A subtle volumetric or shader-based visual layer
- A slow, elegant product rotation
- Responsive camera positioning

Hero headline:

“ENGINEERED
BEYOND
ORDINARY.”

Supporting copy:

“Precision, reimagined. Experience the next dimension of luxury.”

Primary CTA: `EXPLORE AURELION ONE`

Secondary CTA: `DISCOVER THE TECHNOLOGY`

Functional interactions:
- Dragging or pointer movement subtly influences product orientation.
- Scroll input begins a controlled product-story sequence.
- Primary CTA navigates to the product experience.
- Secondary CTA navigates to the technology route.
- The product remains readable and visually prominent across screen sizes.
- A visible scroll indicator guides the visitor into the story.

Do not make pointer movement excessively sensitive. The product should feel weighty and luxurious.

#### Section C: Product reveal

As the user scrolls, animate the product through a choreographed sequence:

1. Begin with a hero-scale product.
2. Transition to a three-quarter perspective.
3. Move into a macro view of its surfaces.
4. Highlight selected material details.
5. Reveal the product silhouette against a brighter lighting environment.
6. Transition into the customization experience.

Use GSAP ScrollTrigger to synchronize camera position, product rotation, material effects, text reveals, lighting, and section progress.

Implement pinning only where it improves the story. Avoid excessive pinning that makes the site difficult to navigate on mobile.

The animation must be smooth, reversible during scrolling, and resilient to viewport resizing.

#### Section D: The materials experience

Create three interactive material explorations:

- Titanium
- Smoked glass
- Ceramic composite

When the visitor selects a material:
- Update the active state clearly.
- Change the product material or a representative 3D surface.
- Adjust roughness, metalness, color, and reflected light as appropriate.
- Display a short material description.
- Animate the material transition naturally.
- Announce the selection accessibly.

If the selected material affects a whole product preset, update the same shared product state everywhere that product is shown.

Do not implement selectors that only change text while pretending to alter the 3D object.

#### Section E: Product customization

Create a premium product configurator with the following controls:

